 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0b08625d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Topic 12: Further differentiation \n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## Chemistry motivation\n",
    "\n",
    "In the monomolecular reaction:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\ce{C2H4Cl2} \\rightarrow \\ce{C2H3Cl} + \\ce{HCl}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "The concentration $c$ of the reactant at any time $t$ is given by:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "c_t = c_0 e^{-kt}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "![](exp.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "where $t$ is the time since the reaction started, and $c_0$ is the concentration of the reactant when the reaction started (at $t = 0$).\n",
    "\n",
    "In this case, it would be of interest to us what the rate of reaction is at any give time $t$ and to do this, we need to be able to differentiate functions other than just polynomials, such an exponentials and trigonometric functions. \n",
    "\n",
    "## Mathematical theory\n",
    "This section will comprise of three subsections, first we will look at rules for differentiating functions you commonly encounter. Following this we will look at the chain rule, which will enable us to differentiate more complicated functions in the third subsection. \n",
    "### Differentiating simple functions\n",
    "#### Exponentials and logorithms\n",
    "Exponentials of the form $e^x$ are very easy to differentiate, in fact they do not change at all, but with the generic base $a$ it is slightly more complicated. \n",
    "\n",
    "```{prf:axiom} Differentiating an exponential function\n",
    "\n",
    "General exponential functions with base $a$:\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(a^x\\right) = a^x \\ln a\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "Whereas exponentials with base $e$ follow the rule:\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(e^x\\right) = e^x\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "```\n",
    "Logorithms, on the other hand, always change, regardless of base.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{prf:axiom} Differentiating a logarithmic function\n",
    "For the natural log $\\ln$ we have \n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\ln(x)\\right) = \\frac{1}{x}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "The general rule (for any arbitrary base $a$) is as follows:\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\log_a(x)\\right) = \\frac{1}{x \\ln(a)}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "**Important:** $x$ must not be $<0$. \n",
    "\n",
    "```{tip} But why? (non-examinable)\n",
    ":class: dropdown\n",
    "First, we apply the change of base formula to allow us to rewrite the base $a$ logarithm using the natural logarithm $\\ln$.\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\log_{a}(x)=\\frac{\\ln (x)}{\\ln (a)}\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "Then we can factor out $\\ln a$ because $a$ is a constant, therefore, $\\frac{1}{\\ln (a)}$⁠ is also a constant and can be pulled outside the derivative.\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\log _{a}(x)\\right]=\\frac{1}{\\ln (a)}\\times \\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\ln (x)\\right]\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "Since the standard derivative of $\\ln(x)$ is $\\frac{1}{x}$⁠, it follows that\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\log_{a}(x)\\right]=\\frac{1}{\\ln (a)}\\times \\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{1}{x\\ln (a)}\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "There are rules for more complicated exponentials and logarithms, which we will look at when we have covered the **chain rule**. \n",
    "\n",
    "#### Trigonometric functions\n",
    "\n",
    "There are many trigonometric functions with associated derivatives, but in this course we will examine the three most commonly used functions. \n",
    "\n",
    "```{prf:axiom} Differentiating $\\sin, \\cos, $ and $\\tan$\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{d}{dx}\\sin(x) = \\cos (x) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{d}{dx}\\cos (x) = - \\sin (x) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{d}{dx}\\tan (x) = \\frac{1}{\\cos^2(x)} \\end{equation*}\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### The chain rule\n",
    "\n",
    "In the previous section we looked at how to differentiate $\\sin(x)$, but what happens if, for example, we have $\\sin(x^2-4x+2)$? \n",
    "\n",
    "Functions such as this are known **composite functions** and are essentially functions inside of other functions. If we let $h(x) = \\sin(x^2-4x+2)$, then we can say that $h(x) = \\textcolor{blue}{f(}\\textcolor{red}{g(x)}\\textcolor{blue}{)}$ such that $f(x) = \\textcolor{blue}{\\sin(x)}$ and $g(x) = \\textcolor{red}{x^2-4x + 2}$. To differentiate functions such as this, we need the **chain rule**.  \n",
    "\n",
    "```{prf:axiom} The chain rule\n",
    "When working with composite function of the form $y = f(g(x))$, let $u = g(x)$ and $y = f(u)$, then \n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy}{du} \\times \\frac{du}{dx} \\end{equation*}\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{seealso} Example\n",
    "Let us go back to $y = \\sin(x^2-4x+2)$. We let $u = x^2-4x+2$ and therefore $y = \\sin(u)$. Using the chain rule, we first need to find $ \\frac{dy}{du}$ and $\\frac{du}{dx}:$\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{du} = \\frac{d}{du}\\sin u = \\cos(u)  \\text{ and }\n",
    "\\newline \\\\[1em] \\frac{du}{dx} = \\frac{d}{dx}(x^2-4x+2) = 2x - 4 \\end{equation*}\n",
    "and using the chain rule we find\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy}{du} \\times \\frac{du}{dx} = (2x - 4)\\cos(u) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "and substituting $u = x^2-4x+2$ back in, we find\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = (2x - 4)\\cos( x^2-4x+2) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Differentiating composite functions\n",
    "Now we know the chain rule, we can look at more general examples of the functions we differentiated in the first section.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Exponentials and logarithms\n",
    "\n",
    "```{prf:axiom} Exponentials\n",
    "When there is a constant $a$ in the power as well as the variable $x$, you implement the chain rule to obtain the rule for differentiating such functions. \n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "   \\text{1. } \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(e^{ax}\\right) = a e^{ax} \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    " \\text{2. }\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(e^{ax+b}\\right) = a e^{ax+b}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\text{3. } \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(a^{kx}\\right) = k a^{kx} \\ln a\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "```{tip} But why? (non examinable)\n",
    ":class: dropdown\n",
    "\n",
    "1. We let $u = ax$ and $y = e^u$. Therefore \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{du}{dx} = a\\text{ and }\\frac{dy}{du} = e^u, \\end{equation*} \n",
    "    and so \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = ae^u = ae^{ax}. \\end{equation*} \n",
    "2. This is the same as (1) except now $u = ax + b$. \n",
    "3. We let $u = kx$ and $y = a^u$. Therefore \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{du}{dx} = k\\text{ and }\\frac{dy}{du} = a^u \\ln a, \\end{equation*} \n",
    "    and so \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = ka^u \\ln a = ka^{kx}\\ln a. \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{prf:axiom} Logorithms\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\text{1. } \\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(kx) = \\frac{1}{x}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\text{2. } \\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(ax + b) = \\frac{a}{ax + b}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "```{tip} But why? (non examinable)\n",
    ":class: dropdown\n",
    "1. You can show this using the chain rule, but actually an easier way is to simply use $\\log(ab) = \\log(a) + \\log(b)$. \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(kx) = \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln k + \\ln x) = 0 + \\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{1}{x} \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "2. We let $u = ax + b$ and $y = \\ln(u)$ and so we have \n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\\frac{du}{dx} = a \\text{ and } \\frac{dy}{du} = \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{ax + b}\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(ax + b) = \\frac{a}{ax + b}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "```{seealso} Examples\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Differentiate $y = e^k - e^{-k}$ with respect to $k$.\n",
    "2. Differentiate $y = \\ln(3x)$ with respect to $x$.\n",
    "3. Differentiate $y = \\ln(4x + 5)$ with respect to $x$.\n",
    "```{tip} Click here for solutions\n",
    ":class: dropdown\n",
    "1. Differentiate $y = e^k - e^{-k}$ with respect to $k$:\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\frac{dy}{dk} = e^k - (-1)e^{-k} = e^k + e^{-k}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Differentiate $y = \\ln(3x)$ with respect to $x$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{x}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Differentiate $y = \\ln(4x + 5)$ with respect to $x$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{4}{4x + 5}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "#### Trigonometric functions\n",
    "```{prf:axiom} Chain rule for trigonometric functions\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin (nx)) = n\\cos (nx)\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos (nx)) = -n\\sin (nx)\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan (nx)) = \\frac{n}{\\cos^2 (nx)}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "```{seealso} Examples - Trigonometric functions\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Differentiate $y = \\sin x + \\cos x$ with respect to $x$.\n",
    "2. Differentiate $y = 4 \\sin 3t$ with respect to $t$.\n",
    "```{tip} Click here for solutions\n",
    ":class: dropdown\n",
    "1. Differentiate $y = \\sin x + \\cos x$ with respect to $x$:\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*}\n",
    "    \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\cos x - \\sin x\n",
    "    \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Differentiate $y = 4 \\sin 3t$ with respect to $t$:\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*}\n",
    "    \\frac{dy}{dt} = 4 \\times 3\\cos 3t = 12\\cos 3t\n",
    "    \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "````\n",
    "### Product rule\n",
    "It may be the case that instead of a composite function, $\\textcolor{blue}{f(}\\textcolor{red}{g(x)}\\textcolor{blue}{)}$, we have a product of two functions $\\textcolor{red}{g(x)}\\textcolor{blue}{f(x)}$ instead. \n",
    "\n",
    "For example: \n",
    "1. $f(x) = \\textcolor{blue}{\\sin(}\\textcolor{red}{x^2-4x + 2}\\textcolor{blue}{)}$ is composite, whereas,\n",
    "2. $f(x) = \\textcolor{blue}{\\sin(x)}\\textcolor{red}{(x^2-4x + 2)}$ is a product of two functions\n",
    "\n",
    "For case 1 we use the chain rule, but for case 2 we will need to use the product rule. \n",
    "\n",
    "```{prf:axiom} Product rule\n",
    "For $u = f(x)$ and $v = g(x)$,\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{d}{dx}uv = u\\frac{dv}{dx} + v\\frac{du}{dx} \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "```{seealso} Example\n",
    "Given $y = \\sin(x)(x^2 - 4x + 2)$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$\n",
    "```{tip} Click here to see solutions\n",
    ":class: dropdown\n",
    "We let $u = \\sin(x)$ and $v = x^2 - 4x +2$. We differentiate both these functions separately, giving\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{du}{dx} = \\cos(x) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "and\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{dv}{dx} = 2x - 4. \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, since \n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{d}{dx}uv = u\\frac{dv}{dx} + v\\frac{du}{dx} \\end{equation*}\n",
    "we find\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sin(x)(2x-4) + (x^2 - 4x +2)\\cos(x) \\\\[1em] \\end{equation*}\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Quotient rule\n",
    "Similarly we might come across quotients of functions (one function divided by another) where they cannot be simplified. There are functions of the form: \n",
    "\\begin{equation*} y = \\frac{\\textcolor{red}{g(x)}}{\\textcolor{blue}{f(x)}}. \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "```{prf:axiom} Quotient rule\n",
    "For $u = f(x)$ and $v = g(x)$\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{d}{dx}\\frac{u}{v} = \\frac{v\\frac{du}{dx} - u\\frac{dv}{dx}}{v^2}  \\end{equation*}\n",
    "```\n",
    "```{seealso} Example\n",
    "Given \n",
    "\\begin{equation*} y = \\frac{\\sin(x)}{(x^2 - 4x + 2)}, \\end{equation*} \n",
    "find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$\n",
    "```{tip} Click here to see solutions\n",
    ":class: dropdown\n",
    "We let $u = \\sin(x)$ and $v = x^2 - 4x +2$. We differentiate both these functions separately, as we did before, giving\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{du}{dx} = \\cos(x) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "and\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{dv}{dx} = 2x - 4. \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, since \n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{d}{dx}\\frac{u}{v} = \\frac{v\\frac{du}{dx} - u\\frac{dv}{dx}}{v^2}  \\end{equation*}\n",
    "we find\n",
    "\\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{(x^2 - 4x +2)\\cos(x) - \\sin(x)(2x-4)}{(x^2 - 4x + 2)^2}. \\\\[1em] \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "### Summary of formulas\n",
    "Below is a reference table for when you are differentiating commonly encountered functions: \n",
    "| Function $y$ | Derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ |\n",
    "| :--- | :--- |\n",
    "| $ax^n$ | $anx^{n-1}$ |\n",
    "| $a^{kx}$| $ka^{kx}\\ln(a)$ |\n",
    "| $e^x$ | $e^x$ |\n",
    "| $e^{ax+b}$ | $ae^{ax+b}$ |\n",
    "| $\\ln x$ | $\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x}$ |\n",
    "| $\\ln(ax + b)$ | $\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{ax+b}$ |\n",
    "| $\\log_a (x)$ | $\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x \\ln(a)}$ |\n",
    "| $\\sin (x)$ | $\\cos (x)$ |\n",
    "| $\\sin (nx)$ | $n\\cos (nx)$ |\n",
    "| $\\cos (x)$ | $-\\sin (x)$ |\n",
    "| $\\cos (nx)$ | $-n\\sin (nx)$ |\n",
    "| $\\tan (x)$ | $ \\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\cos^2 (x)}$ |\n",
    "| $\\tan (nx)$ | $\\displaystyle \\frac{n}{\\cos^2 (nx)}$ |\n",
    "\n",
    "```{seealso} Exercises\n",
    "1. Differentiate the following functions with respect to $x$: <br> \n",
    "    (a) $y = x^3 + 2x$ <br>\n",
    "    (b) $y = \\sin(5x)$ <br>\n",
    "    (c) $y = \\frac{3}{x^2}$ <br> \n",
    "    (d) $y = e^{4x}$ <br>\n",
    "    (e) $y = 3\\cos(2x)$ <br>\n",
    "    (f) $y = \\frac{1}{5}x^5$ <br>\n",
    "---\n",
    "2. For each of the functions below find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ by using an appropriate rule: <br>\n",
    "    (a) $y = \\frac{6+x^2}{x^3+1}$ <br>\n",
    "    (b) $y = x\\ln x$ <br>\n",
    "    (c) $y = \\sin^2 x$ <br>\n",
    "    (d) $y = \\sin(x^2)$ <br>\n",
    "    (e) $y = e^x \\sin x$ <br>\n",
    "    (f) $y = x^2 e^{-x}$ <br>\n",
    " ---   \n",
    "3. Apply the chain rule to find the derivative of: <br>\n",
    "    (a) $y = e^{x\\sin x}$ <br>\n",
    "    (b) $y = \\ln(4x^3 - 2)$ <br>\n",
    "    (c) $y = -\\cos^2(2x^2)$ <br>\n",
    "---\n",
    "4. Find the first, second and third derivatives of the following functions: <br>\n",
    "    (a) $y = \\frac{1}{x}$ <br>\n",
    "    (b) $y = N\\sin(ax)$ where $a$ is a constant <br>\n",
    "    *Extension: Find a general expression for the $n^{\\text{th}}$ derivative, $\\frac{d^ny}{dx^n}$, for the above functions.*\n",
    " ---   \n",
    "5. An important quantity that arises in molecular orbital theory is the overlap integral, $S$. For the $\\text{H}_2^+$ ion, $S$ is described by the function: \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} S(r) = \\frac{e^{-r}}{3}(3 + 3r + r^2) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "    where $r$ is an independent variable, $-\\infty < r < \\infty$. <br>\n",
    "    (a) Derive an expression for $\\frac{dS(r)}{dr}$. <br>\n",
    "    (b) Determine the stationary point(s) of this function and classify the stationary point(s) as a local maximum, local minimum, or point of inflection.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{tip} Click here for solutions\n",
    ":class: dropdown \n",
    "\n",
    "1. (a) If $y = x^3 + 2x,$ \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = 3x^2 + 2 \\end{equation*}\n",
    "    (b) If $y = \\sin(5x),$\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = 5\\cos(5x) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "    (c) If $y = \\frac{3}{x^2} = 3x^{-2},$\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = -6x^{-3} = -\\frac{6}{x^3} \\end{equation*} \n",
    "    (d) If $y = e^{4x},$\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = 4e^{4x} \\end{equation*}\n",
    "    (e) If $y = 3\\cos(2x),$\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\newline \\frac{dy}{dx} = -6\\sin(2x) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "    (f) If $y = \\frac{1}{5}x^5,$ \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = x^4 \\end{equation*}\n",
    "---\n",
    "2. (a) For $y = \\frac{6+x^2}{x^3+1}$ we use the quotient rule: \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{(2x)(x^3+1) - (6+x^2)(3x^2)}{(x^3+1)^2} \\newline \\\\[1em] = \\frac{2x^4 + 2x - 18x^2 - 3x^4}{(x^3+1)^2} \\newline \\\\[1em] = \\frac{-x^4 - 18x^2 + 2x}{(x^3+1)^2} \\end{equation*}\n",
    "    (b) For $y = x\\ln x$, we have two functions $u = x$ and $v = \\ln x$ multiplied together so we use the product rule. \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = (1)\\ln x + x\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\ln x + 1 \\end{equation*}\n",
    "    (c) For $y = \\sin^2 x = (\\sin x)^2$, we have $u^2$ such that $u = \\sin x$ therefore we have a composite function and use the chain rule: \n",
    "\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = 2\\sin x \\cos x (\\text{or } \\sin 2x) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "    (d) For $y = \\sin(x^2)$, we use the chain rule, \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\cos(x^2) \\cdot 2x = 2x\\cos(x^2) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "    (e) Given $y = e^x \\sin x$, we need to use the product rule, such that $u = e^x$ and $v = \\sin x$\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = e^x \\sin x + e^x \\cos x = e^x(\\sin x + \\cos x)\\end{equation*}\n",
    "    (f) For $y = x^2 e^{-x}$ we use a combination of the product & chain rules, such that $u = x^2$ and $v = e^{-x}$. \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*}\\frac{dy}{dx} = (2x)e^{-x} + x^2(-e^{-x}) = xe^{-x}(2 - x) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Applying the chain rule: <br>\n",
    "    (a) Let $u = x\\sin x \\implies \\frac{du}{dx} = \\sin x + x\\cos x$ (using the product rule).\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{x\\sin x}(\\sin x + x\\cos x) \\end{equation*} <br>\n",
    "    (b) Let $u = 4x^3 - 2 \\implies \\frac{du}{dx} = 12x^2$.\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{12x^2}{4x^3 - 2} = \\frac{6x^2}{2x^3 - 1} \\end{equation*}\n",
    "    (c) $y = -\\cos^2(2x^2) = -[\\cos(2x^2)]^2$ \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} \\frac{dy}{dx} = -2\\cos(2x^2) \\times [-\\sin(2x^2)] \\times 4x \\newline \\\\[1em] = 8x\\cos(2x^2)\\sin(2x^2) \\quad (\\text{or } 4x\\sin(4x^2)) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "4. Finding the first, second and third derivatives of the following functions gives us: \n",
    "    (a) $y = \\frac{1}{x} = x^{-1}$ \n",
    "    * $ \\frac{dy}{dx} = -x^{-2} = -\\frac{1}{x^2}$\n",
    "    * $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 2x^{-3} = \\frac{2}{x^3}$\n",
    "    * $\\frac{d^3y}{dx^3} = -6x^{-4} = -\\frac{6}{x^4}$\n",
    "    * *Extension:* $\\frac{d^ny}{dx^n} = \\frac{(-1)^n n!}{x^{n+1}}$ <br>\n",
    "    (b) $y = N\\sin(ax)$ where $a$ is a constant \n",
    "    * $\\frac{dy}{dx} = Na\\cos(ax)$\n",
    "    * $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = -Na^2\\sin(ax)$\n",
    "    * $\\frac{d^3y}{dx^3} = -Na^3\\cos(ax)$\n",
    "    * *Extension:* $\\frac{d^ny}{dx^n} = Na^n\\sin\\left(ax + \\frac{n\\pi}{2}\\right)$\n",
    "---\n",
    "5. (a) Deriving an expression for $\\frac{dS(r)}{dr}$ by applying the product rule with $u = \\frac{1}{3}e^{-r}$ and $v = 3 + 3r + r^2$ we get:\n",
    "\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*}\\frac{dS(r)}{dr} = \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}e^{-r}\\right)(3 + 3r + r^2) + \\left(\\frac{1}{3}e^{-r}\\right)(3 + 2r) \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*}\\frac{dS(r)}{dr} = \\frac{e^{-r}}{3}\\left(-3 - 3r - r^2 + 3 + 2r\\right) \\newline \\\\[1em] =  -\\frac{e^{-r}}{3}(r^2 + r) = - \\frac{r(r + 1)e^{-r}}{3}.  \\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "    (b) to determine and classify the stationary point(s) we set $\\frac{dS(r)}{dr} = 0$:\n",
    "    \\begin{equation*}-\\frac{r(r + 1)e^{-r}}{3} = 0 \\end{equation*}\n",
    "    Since $e^{-r} > 0$ for all real $r$, we solve \n",
    "    \\begin{equation*} r(r + 1) = 0 \\end{equation*} \n",
    "    which tells us $r = 0$ or $r = -1$.\n",
    "    Next we find the second derivative $\\frac{d^2S(r)}{dr^2}$ using the product rule on $-\\frac{1}{3}(r^2 + r)e^{-r}$:\n",
    "     \\begin{equation*}\\\\[1em] \\frac{d^2S(r)}{dr^2} = -\\frac{1}{3}\\left[(2r + 1)e^{-r} - (r^2 + r)e^{-r}\\right] = \\frac{e^{-r}}{3}(r^2 - r - 1)\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "     * At $r = 0$:<br>\n",
    "       $S(0) = \\frac{e^{0}}{3}(3 + 0 + 0) = 1$ tells us there is a stationary point at $(0, 1)$. <br>\n",
    "       $S''(0) = \\frac{e^{0}}{3}(0 - 0 - 1) = -\\frac{1}{3} < 0 $ means it is a local maximum.\n",
    "     * At $r = -1$: <br>\n",
    "       $S(-1) = \\frac{e^{1}}{3}(3 - 3 + 1) = \\frac{e}{3}$ means there is a stationary point at $\\left(-1, \\frac{e}{3}\\right)$. <br>\n",
    "       $S''(-1) = \\frac{e^{1}}{3}((-1)^2 - (-1) - 1) = \\frac{e}{3} > 0 $ tells us it is a local minimum.\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
